# kube_for prints the kubeconfig that ANSWERS for an endpoint, never the one
# whose filename looks right. ~/.kube here holds op-usxpress-dev.yaml pointing
# at cloud QA EKS, and two files per on-prem endpoint with different auth.
kube_for() {
  local ep="$1" f s
  for f in "$HOME"/.kube/*.yaml "$HOME"/.kube/*.yml; do
    [[ -f "$f" ]] || continue
    s=$(kubectl --kubeconfig="$f" config view -o jsonpath='{.clusters[*].cluster.server}' 2>/dev/null)
    [[ $(echo "$s" | wc -w) -eq 1 ]] || continue
    [[ "$s" == *"$ep:6443"* ]] || continue
    kubectl --kubeconfig="$f" get --raw /version >/dev/null 2>&1 && { printf '%s' "$f"; return 0; }
  done
  return 1
}

banner "Make op-usxpress-prod production ready"

